  "query_preview": "..A weak cold front is moving through Arizona this\nafternoon leading to showers and isolated thunderstorms. Aloft a\nshortwave is moving into Utah. The cold front is currently \nsplitting Arizona in half with most shower activity from Phoenix\nnorthward. Current radar does show a couple isolated showers \nover Pinal and Graham Counties. Expect continued isolated shower\nactivity through the evening mainly north and east of Tucson. \nAny rain accumulations will be very light, below 0.1\". Winds \nwill also be a little breezy with speeds 12-18 mph and gusts \n25-30 mph. \n\nAfter today the shortwave moves into the northern Great Plains \nand the next upper level low begins to move into the Pacific \nNorthwest. It is currently a fairly strong closed low, but will\nopen up and move southeastward over the next few days. Friday \ninto Saturday it will be an open wave and move through northern \nArizona or southern Utah, still uncertainty on how far south it \nwill travel. Right now southeast Arizona is expected to be dry \nwhen this system moves through with rain chances staying further\nnorth. Winds may also be a little breezy Friday afternoon. \nHowever the upper air pattern stays progressive with ensembles \nbringing another trough through the region early next week. \nThis trough looks to have a more southerly trajectory and could \nbring rain chances Monday. NBM PoPs are currently 15-30 percent.\n\nTemperatures will follow the roller coaster of below normal to \nslight above normal for the week due to the passing weather \nsystems.",
